# The working corpus, the crash artifacts, and the build tree.
#
# **None of these are committed, and the corpus is the one worth explaining.** libFuzzer grows
# `corpus/<target>/` without limit: every input that reaches a new edge is written there, so a few
# minutes of fuzzing leaves thousands of files and a long run leaves hundreds of megabytes. Committing
# that would put a machine-generated, machine-specific, unbounded blob in the history of a repository
# whose whole documentation argument is that a reader can follow it.
#
# The seeds ARE committed (`seeds/`), and the difference is the point: a seed is a chosen input with a
# reason, a corpus entry is an artifact of one run on one machine.
#
# Crash artifacts are not committed either, and that is a discipline rather than an omission: when a
# target finds a crash, the input becomes a host test in the crate that owns the bug, where it runs in
# milliseconds on every `script/test` forever. See notes/fuzzing.md.
corpus/
artifacts/
coverage/
target/
